E[7]={'lecture16':{
5:[('The scatterplot holds all **525** vowel tokens, **105** of each vowel. Each dot is one recording, placed by its **F1** along the bottom and its **F2** up the side, both in hertz.',['These are the same two numbers the network receives as input.','The color gives the correct word for that recording, so the plot pairs every input with its answer.']),
 ('Five colors match the five outputs.',['Red is **i (dean)**, magenta is **ae (Dan)**, blue is **u (dune)**, cyan is **a (Don)**, and green is **schwa (done)**.','Each vowel forms a cloud, because the same vowel comes out with different formants on different tokens.','No tokens were removed, so a few stray dots sit far from their clouds.']),
 ('Two vowels stand apart.',['Red **i** sits high on F2, mostly above about 2000 Hz, with a low F1.','Magenta **ae** spreads out toward high F1, past 800 Hz.']),
 ('Three vowels overlap.',['Blue **u**, cyan **a** and green **schwa** crowd together in the lower middle, roughly 300 to 800 Hz in F1 and 800 to 1600 Hz in F2.','A token in that region could belong to any of the three.']),
 ('The overlap limits any classifier that sees only F1 and F2.',['Tokens that land in the same spot look identical to the network, whatever their label.','No rule based on these two numbers can label all of them correctly, which is why the trained networks stay well below perfect.'])],
15:[('The code trains the **2 → 3 → 2 → 4 → 5** network on the vowel data, starting from one random **initialization** fixed by **torch.manual_seed(0)**.'.replace(' → ',' to '),['Every layer is **nn.Linear** with **bias=False**, and no activations sit between layers, so this is the all-linear network.','The formants are divided by **1000**, turning hertz into kilohertz and keeping the inputs small.','**argmax(1)** turns each one-hot target row into the number of its correct class.']),
 ('Training uses **CrossEntropyLoss** and plain **SGD** with a **learning rate of 0.1** for **2,000 epochs**.',['Each epoch clears old gradients, computes the loss on all the tokens together, backpropagates and takes one step.','So every epoch is one **full-batch update**.']),
 ('After each update the code measures accuracy without tracking gradients.',['The network’s answer for a token is its largest score.','Accuracy is the percentage of tokens where that answer is the correct class.','The tokens are the same **525** used for training, so this is **training accuracy**.']),
 ('The curve starts at about **20%**, the level for one of five equally common vowels.',['It stays flat for about 200 updates, then climbs into the 40s.','Near update 390 it drops sharply toward 20% for a moment, then recovers.','After about 450 updates it barely moves, ending at **44.95%**, which is 236 of 525 tokens.']),
 ('The plotted run comes from an equivalent **NumPy** program.',['PyTorch was unavailable where the curve was made, so the same network, loss, scaling and updates were run in NumPy.','The code gives the PyTorch version of the procedure, and the curve gives the result of running it.'])],
16:[('The same training runs ten times, with **seeds 0 through 9**.',['Each run builds a fresh model and a fresh SGD optimizer.','Only the **initialization**, the random starting weights, changes from run to run.','Data, scaling, loss, learning rate and the 2,000 full-batch updates stay identical.']),
 ('Each colored line is one seed, and all ten start near **20%**.',['Some leave 20% within the first 100 updates, and others stay flat for 200 or more.','Some drop back toward 20% for a short time before recovering.','The highest accuracy along the way is close to **47%** in some runs.']),
 ('The lines then come together.',['The fastest runs settle within about 350 updates, and the slowest take about 1,000.','After that the curves overlap, and **all ten finish at 44.95%**.']),
 ('Different starting weights take different paths but reach the same end.',['Trying more seeds or picking the best one would not raise the final accuracy here.','The limit comes from the network and the overlapping vowel data, not from unlucky starting weights.']),
 ('The curves are executed **NumPy** reference runs.',['Matching seed numbers in NumPy and PyTorch do not give matching starting weights.','A PyTorch run with the same seed would not trace exactly the same line, but the pattern of varied paths and a shared end point is what the runs produced.'])]},
'lecture17':{
2:[('The vowel network returns as a picture of what neural networks did from **1943 to 1986**.',['Two formants come in, weighted connections carry them through three hidden layers, and five outputs respond for done, dune, dean, Don and Dan.','The weights are the same illustrative signed weights used for forward propagation and backpropagation.']),
 ('**McCulloch and Pitts (1943)** mark the start.',['Their paper is **“A Logical Calculus of the Ideas Immanent in Nervous Activity.”**','It begins from the **“all-or-none” character of nervous activity**, a neuron either fires or not, and treats neural events with propositional logic.']),
 ('The two **Parallel Distributed Processing** volumes of **1986** mark the end.',['They made networks of many simple connected units, trained by adjusting weights, widely used.']),
 ('**Given some static vector of formants, the NN could make a decision about a few possible categories.**',['A static vector is one fixed list of numbers measured at one moment, here F1 and F2.','The network maps that single input onto one of five words.']),
 ('**Problem. Human cognition is far more complex.**',['Speech unfolds in time, and a sentence is a long sequence in which what comes next depends on what came before.','A network that maps one fixed vector to a few labels cannot keep track of earlier inputs, which is the gap recurrent networks and then LLMs fill.'])],
3:[('In **1985, Michael Jordan developed recurrent neural networks** to make sense of **speech production**.',['That work led toward **LLMs** and then **agentic AI**.','The photo on the right is Michael Jordan.']),
 ('The network has four kinds of units.',['**Plan units** take outside input that says which sequence to produce.','**Hidden units** combine information, and **output units** give the response.','**State units** receive the outputs back along the long curved arrows, and each state unit also feeds back to itself.']),
 ('Those loops make the network **recurrent**.',['The state units hold a trace of what the network just produced.','That trace feeds the hidden units along with the plan, so each output depends on the plan and on the network’s own recent outputs.','A feedforward classifier has no such memory, since each input passes through once.']),
 ('The plot, **Performance on /istrstry/**, tracks the output over 32 time steps.',['Each row is one articulatory feature, **Voice, Place, Sonorant, Sibilant, Nasal, Height, Back and Round**.','Sibilant rises in bumps where the s sounds occur, while Nasal and Height stay nearly flat.']),
 ('**Round** rises early and stays high for most of the sequence.',['Instead of switching on and off for each sound, the feature spreads across neighbors.','Feeding outputs back makes successive outputs blend smoothly, and that idea of using past outputs carries on into today’s language models.'])],
4:[('The page builds from **one neuron** to an **agentic system** in four steps.',['A vector **x** comes into one neuron, each part is multiplied by a weight **w**, and the products are summed into one number **y**.','**Vector in, one number out**, and that sum is an inner product.']),
 ('A **network** stacks layers of 4, 3, 4 and 5 units joined by **W₁, W₂ and W₃**.',['Each W turns one vector into the next.','The last layer is a **decision vector**, one value per possible answer, so this is **vector in, decision vector out**, like the vowel classifier.']),
 ('An **LLM** takes **vectors 1 through 15** in at the bottom, one per token, and passes them up through **5 layers** of W blocks.',['The six colors of W stand for six different kinds of weight sets.','At the top comes **vector 16**, drawn as a bar chart labeled **PDF**, a probability distribution over the next token.','It is called **a dynamical system with many vectors in and one decision vector out**.']),
 ('An **agentic system is a graph of LLMs**.',['Separate LLMs run in order, **Outline, Write, Critic, Revise and Final**.','A **loop** between Critic and Revise lets the critique send work back for more revision before the final model finishes.']),
 ('The bottom line names the two operations under every step.',['**Inference is inner products**, weighted sums passed from layer to layer.','**Learning is SGD plus DP**, gradient descent driven by the dynamic-programming bookkeeping of backpropagation.'])],
5:[('The page starts with **15 vectors, x₁ to x₁₅**, an empty slot for **x₁₆**, and the question **vectors of what?**',['An LLM receives a sequence of vectors and predicts the next one.','Four reveals fill the same arrangement with four kinds of sequence.']),
 ('First, **a sentence cut into 15 words**.',['“The cat that the dog chased ran up the old tree and sat on the ___.”','Each vector stands for one word, and **x₁₆ is the missing word**.','Filling it in needs words far back in the sentence, since the cat is the one sitting.']),
 ('Next, **a spectrogram cut into 15 frames**, then **an image cut into 16 patches**.',['The spectrogram runs from 0 to 5 kHz up the side with time to the right, and **x₁₆ is the next frame**.','The image of a house, a tree and the sun is read row by row, and **x₁₆ is the missing bottom-right patch**.']),
 ('Last, **a protein, the insulin B chain**, read as **15 amino acids**.',['The letters are F V N Q H L C G S H L V E A L, drawn folding into a helix.','**x₁₆ is the next residue**, which is **Y**.']),
 ('**The LLM only ever sees vectors.**',['Words, sounds, pixels and molecules all become sequences of vectors.','So **one machine asks one question, what is x₁₆?**, whatever the source of the sequence.'])],
6:[('The page asks **which sequences can be English words**, in three rows.',['Row one has twenty **one-syllable words**, each a single consonant, a single vowel and a single consonant, such as Don, mat, sit, cup, fish and ring.','Consonants are dark, vowels red, and a silent e grey.','**A simple syllable in English can have a consonant, then a vowel, then a consonant.**']),
 ('Row two adds **clusters**, groups of consonants.',['Clusters before the vowel are **onsets**, as in split, flat, black, glad and stop.','Clusters after the vowel are **codas**, as in desk, box, help, milk and lamp.','Each real word has a starred impossible twin, such as *sblit, *lfat, *bnack, *dlad, *tsop, *desb, *bokp, *hepl, *mikl and *lapm.']),
 ('Many twins use the same letters in the other order.',['flat and *lfat, help and *hepl, lamp and *lapm differ only in order.','**Order matters**, and the pattern mirrors around the vowel.','**Only certain sequences are allowed before the vowel and after the vowel.**']),
 ('Row three has **words of many syllables**, with dots marking the breaks.',['ba·na·na, win·dow, pump·kin, nap·kin, At·lan·tic, ath·lete, ad·mit and spa·ghet·ti are real words.','t then l, th then l, and d then m are fine across a break, but cannot start a word, so *tlantic, *thlete and *dmit are impossible.','ng can end a syllable, as in ring, but never start one, so *ngapkin is impossible.']),
 ('**A long word is a chain of syllables**, each obeying the same rules.',['**t·l is fine across a break, but tl cannot start a syllable.**','This knowledge of allowed sound sequences is **phonotactics**, and English speakers have it without being taught.'])],
7:[('The pop quiz asks how **phonotactic knowledge can be revealed through GPT learning**.',['Each item is the start of an English word, and the task is to **guess the next letter**.','The starts are ra_, sp_, k_, skr_, m_, bram_, b_, f_ and sh_.','People agree quickly, and how much they agree changes from item to item.']),
 ('After a vowel the choices open up.',['After **ra_**, letters like t, n, r and l are all likely, since **after a vowel almost anything** can follow.']),
 ('At the start of a word the choices narrow.',['After **sp_**, **b_** and **f_**, only **l, r or a vowel**, never *spn, *bn or *fn.','After **k_**, a vowel or **n** as in knee, never *kt.','After **sh_**, a vowel or **r** as in shrimp, never *shb.']),
 ('Some starts leave almost no choice.',['After **skr_** and after **m_**, only a vowel, never *skrl or *mb.','After **bram_**, b is nearly forced, as in bramble, though a new syllable could also start there.']),
 ('**Guessing the next letter is predicting x₁₆.**',['A **GPT trained only on this game learns English sounds and spelling**, because it must match these patterns.','Its predicted distribution is flat after fa_ and sharply peaked after skr_.'])]}}
